## 4. Which tensors are which precision, and what the activations are (derived)

From `hf_quant_config.json`, confirmed against the dtype of every tensor:

| what | stored as | computed in |
|---|---|---|
| routed experts, layers {{ W.expert_groups.nvfp4.layers[0] }}-{{ W.expert_groups.nvfp4.layers[-1] }} | NVFP4: two E2M1 codes per byte, one E4M3 scale per {{ W.quantization.nvfp4_weights.weight_block_shape[1] }} values along the input, one FP32 scale per expert | NVFP4 weights × **NVFP4 activations** |
| routed experts, layers {{ W.expert_groups.mxfp8.layers[0] }}-{{ W.expert_groups.mxfp8.layers[-1] }} | MXFP8: E4M3 values, one power-of-two scale per {{ W.quantization.mxfp8_weights.required_activation_quantization.block_size }} values | MXFP8 weights × **MXFP8 activations** |
| attention projections (query, key, value, relative, gate, sink, output), router, convolutions, norms, embedding, output projection, vision, draft | BF16 | BF16; the output projection returns FP32 |
| attention-residual queries, router bias | FP32 | FP32 |

The activation format is the answer to "FP4 or FP8?": it follows the weights. The NVFP4 expert GEMMs
quantize their inputs to `{{ W.quantization.nvfp4_weights.required_activation_quantization.format }}` in
{{ W.quantization.nvfp4_weights.required_activation_quantization.block_size }}-value blocks with
`{{ W.quantization.nvfp4_weights.required_activation_quantization.scale_dtype }}` block scales, a
**{{ W.quantization.nvfp4_weights.required_activation_quantization.amax_type | replace("_", "-") }}** second-level scale, and the
`{{ W.quantization.nvfp4_weights.required_activation_quantization.scale_selection_rule }}` rule: each block's
maximum is scaled to 6 or to 4, whichever reconstructs the block with the lower mean absolute error
(`vllm-blog`). Two consequences for hardware:

* The second-level scale is per token, so a token's quantization does not depend on what else is
  in the batch. That is a precondition for the batch-invariance check in the silicon section.
* The published engines keep expert parallelism equal to tensor parallelism because an NVFP4
  expert cannot be split across devices: its activation scales must cover the expert's full input
  width (`sglang-recipe`).

NVIDIA's definition of NVFP4 matches what the checkpoint stores: E2M1 values, one E4M3 scale per 16
values, one FP32 scale per tensor, 4.5 bits per value (`nvfp4-blog`). In this checkpoint the "tensor"
for the FP32 scale is one expert.

## 5. The four kinds of layer (derived)

| attention | experts | layers | dense weights | one expert | 8 routed experts | weight MACs per token | resident |
|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|
{% for k in KINDS %}
| {{ k.attention }} | {{ k.expert_precision | upper }} | {{ k.layers | length }} | {{ n(k.dense_params / 1e6, 1) }}M, {{ mb(k.dense_bytes) }} | {{ mb(k.expert_bytes_each, 1) }} | {{ mb(k.routed_bytes_per_token) }} | {{ n(k.weight_macs_per_token / 1e6, 0) }}M | {{ gb(k.resident_bytes, 2) }} |
{% endfor %}

"Dense weights" are shared by every token: the attention projections and the router. "8 routed
experts" is what one token reads from the MoE. Full-attention layers carry slightly more dense
weight because their key and value convolutions depend on the input. The per-layer table for all
{{ C.layers }} layers is in `results/phase0_workload.json` under `layers`.

Attention itself costs {{ n(W.attention.macs_per_row_per_layer_per_context_token) }} MACs per layer for
every token of context it looks at. In the {{ C.full_layers }} full-attention layers that is
{{ n(W.attention.full_macs_per_row_per_context_token) }} MACs per context token, so attention
arithmetic overtakes weight arithmetic at about {{ n(W.attention.context_where_full_attention_macs_equal_weight_macs, -3) }}
tokens of context.

